"The program provides deterministic, closed-form, bounded parameter proposals for two native predictor interfaces. For ridge (shared linear weights), the native prediction is baseline + features @ (newweights - weights). We treat the correction delta = newweights - weights as the unknown and solve a ridge-regularized least-squares problem on the supplied feedback rows: minimize ||features @ delta - (target - baseline)||^2 + lambda * ||delta||^2, with an additional hard norm bound applied after solving. Because features include intercept coordinates, no separate offset is added. The closed form is delta = (X^T X + lambda I)^{-1} X^T r, computed via a small symmetric solve with diagonal jitter for numerical stability; if the system is ill-conditioned we fall back to a scaled gradient-like closed-form step (X^T r / (diag(X^T X)+lambda)) rather than iterative optimization. Candidate recipes vary lambda and the hard bound radius, giving 4 candidates (excluding no-op). For xgboost, each tree contributes a leaf value additively; residual = target - original prediction. For each tree and each supported leaf id, we compute the mean residual of rows falling in that leaf, then shrink it by a factor n_leaf/(n_leaf + kappa) and clip to a bounded magnitude. This is a closed-form, finite, bounded leaf increment. Candidate recipes vary kappa and the clip bound, giving 3 candidates. All functions are pure: they copy inputs, never mutate them, and return finite floats. The host supplies features, baseline, target, weights, residual, leaf_ids, and tree_count; the program supplies only candidate settings and the numerical update rule. The same fixed program is reused across feedback folds and seeds; no data-dependent candidate expansion or query-guided selection occurs."
